9. Failover Scenario

Assume Node 1 is the current primary.

If Node 1 fails, EFM performs its normal failure detection and promotes an eligible standby according to the existing EFM configuration.

For example:

BEFORE

Node 1 → PRIMARY
Node 2 → STANDBY
Node 3 → STANDBY
Node 4 → STANDBY

After failover:

AFTER

Node 1 → NOT PRIMARY / FAILED
Node 2 → PRIMARY
Node 3 → STANDBY
Node 4 → STANDBY

The EFM health checks consequently become:

Node 1 → HTTP 404
Node 2 → HTTP 200
Node 3 → HTTP 404
Node 4 → HTTP 404

The Load Balancer then identifies Node 2 as the active PostgreSQL backend.

                         QLIK
                           |
                           |
                    postgres-ha.company.com
                           |
                           ▼
                    Load Balancer
                           |
                           |
                           ▼
                    Node 2 :5432
                    PostgreSQL PRIMARY

Qlik continues using the same FQDN.

No Qlik configuration change is required.

---

10. Important Connection Behavior

The solution provides automatic routing for new PostgreSQL connections.

An existing TCP connection that was established to a failed PostgreSQL primary cannot be transferred by the Load Balancer to another PostgreSQL server.

Therefore, during an actual database failure:

Existing Qlik session
        |
        X
Connection lost

Qlik must establish a new connection.

The Qlik connector/job configuration should therefore be tested to confirm its connection retry/reconnect behavior.

The architecture guarantees endpoint stability; application-level session recovery must be validated separately.

